A||||21||Shiner||Tires, Tires, Tires!||||5||
Z||000000||Shiner||02-03-2000||06:24 PM||||The GT will be needing a new set of meat this spring, and I have been looking at a bunch of different brands.  Anybody have personal experience with Dunlop SP Sport 8000, Pirelli P6000 Sport Veloce, Firestone Firehawk SH30, or BFG Touring T/A VR4's?  Does anybody have any recomendations not listed above?  Thanks a ton!<p>[This message has been edited by Shiner (edited 02-03-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Eric||02-03-2000||06:38 PM||||I have a set of yoko A509s.  I think LowCG uses them too.  They're really soft and wear fast, but they provide a heck of a ride.  Price isn't too bad either.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||02-03-2000||07:08 PM||||I always ran Toyo's on my GT... had good luck with them.  Yokohama makes a nice tire.  They really impressed me at 24 Hours of Daytona several years back... not that I would buy a tire solely on racing results  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000003||Eddie.H||02-03-2000||07:15 PM||||Shiner the  (s) is the speed rating you DO not want an S rating tire I love dunlops Check out dunlop D60 A2's I love them they have an H rating and tread 230 wear thats real good.  PS check them out||||reg||6
Z||000004||olympic||02-03-2000||07:41 PM||||    Michelin Pilot MXX3 are supose to be great all around, especially in the rain.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||02-03-2000||07:53 PM||||I've been real happy with my Toyos.  PROXES H4 on my '87 GT.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||02-03-2000||08:18 PM||||I've got 4 mismatched tires on my '86.  Let's see...Hoosier, Goodyear, BFG, and General.  Traction sucks as does wear.  <B>DON'T BUY MIS MATCHED TIRES!  GET THE SAME BRAND FOR ALL 4!!</B>  Just a quick word of advice.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||02-03-2000||10:12 PM||||Monkey,<P>I guess it depends on what type you put on there.  If you put one slick on the rear and one performance all weather tire and then another on one of the fronts and an aquatred on the other front... You would have the ULTIMATE handling.  Roadholding goes up further if you have cosmetic mismatches such as 1 or 2 white walls or raised white letters out on alternating ends of the car.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000008||Shiner||02-03-2000||11:55 PM||||Actually, the car came with 3 different tires from the previous owner!  The fronts are worn out Goodyear Eagle GT2's.  One rear is a Firehawk SH30 (that tire looks really nice!) and a Kelly Charger on the other rear. ||||reg||9
Z||000009||fieroshop||02-04-2000||12:38 AM||||It really doesn't matter what tire you get. Everybody has their own preference as you will also in due time. What maters is that you get all four tires the same brand and the correct size. Also having a GT make sure that you get an H or higher rating for your car.Just my professional .02 cents worth Just ||||reg||7
Z||000010||fieroshop||02-04-2000||12:39 AM||||It really doesn't matter what tire you get. Everybody has their own preference as you will also in due time. What maters is that you get all four tires the same brand and the correct size. Also having a GT make sure that you get an H or higher rating for your car.Just my professional .02 cents worth.||||reg||7
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||01:06 AM||||If you are realisticly one of those people who drives their Fiero from point A to B and never see a curve beyond the left turn lane, you can get S rated. Before the last modification in codes an S rating was a tire speed test to 112 miles an hour. (Im not sure what it is now.) If it has a high treadwear than you will be fine for tires for a long time. There is no need to pay for tires that you will never use to their potential. <BR>There, Im light 2 pennies now.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shiner||02-04-2000||02:10 AM||||Don't worry, I was not considering an S rated tire, PERIOD. DOT.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I don't want to buy tires with a speed rating which I will exceed.  I know it's not PC and all, but I do occasionaly like to run the GT up past the buck mark.  My priorities, in order from most desired are: <BR>1) Very good handling<BR>2) Agressive looking tread pattern <BR>3) A decent treadwear rating, because I am not made of money.<P>I LOVE how the Firehawk SH30's look, they are H rated, cheap, and come in the correct sizes.  I had a flat awhile back though, on the ONE SH30 on the car (lol).  Couldn't find any punctures, so the tire shop guys put a bunch of bead sealer on, and it has been fine since.  Kind of strange...||||reg||7
Z||000013||RossT||02-04-2000||02:44 AM||||Shiner, One thing to consider:  I have been having problems with my speed rated eagle gt+4 tires on my original gt rims, slowly leaking air.  The colder the temp, the greater the leakage.  No visible punture on the tires, but when you put water on the bead you would see leakage from behing the wheel balancing weights. When the tire would warm up inside, the leakage would stop. This was very frustrating, only one tire was doing it.  I took it to several tire shops.  The first one used bead sealer, but it started to leak after a week.  The next place removed the weight and put to smaller ones on each side of the original.  That worked for a week also.  My tires only have 700 miles on them (rims also) no nicks or coroasion on the inside. Finally I took it to my regualar mechanic and got a good response:  aluminum rims+speed rated tires+pound on weights+cold weather= do not mix. He said that the high speed rated tires have a very stiff side wall and  bead. The pound on weights protrude to far down into the bead and in cold weather the aluminum contract more than the hard rubber allowing the potential for air leaks. His solution was to first remove the weights and see if the leakage stops. I did and after 3 weeks of North Dakota cold winter, no leaks. I took the tire to another place and found I guy who said that this is a comon problem and the solution was to use stick on weights on the inside of the rim. He said they only work on aluminum rims that are clean on the inside before the weight is applied.  He said that they DO NOT COME OFF in bad weather, in fact, the clamp on weights are more likely to come off. When I get a chance I am going to do the other 3 tires the same way. The weights are on the inside, thus giving a nice clean look to the outside. If I get a chance I will post a pic of what the inside looks like. The last tire shop guy also said that there are pound on wieghts that don't protrude as far in as the regular pound on weights, but he has had better luck with the stick on weights.<P>Ross ||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||02-04-2000||07:46 AM||||Shiner, I looked into the tire thing recently because I'm getting a new set for the Batmobile this spring. I decided on the BF Goodrich Comp T/A. Maybe not the VERY best tire on the market, but they have been ranked quite favorably in a couple articles I've seen, plus they're not as expensive as some of the other high speed rated performance tires I've seen.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Scott2M4||02-04-2000||09:47 AM||||Allow me to put a slight twist on things ...<P>I'm in the market for tires too.  However, I don't care about cornering and high speed capabilities.  Plain and simple, I want an inexpensive, all-season tire with an average mileage warranty and <B>LARGE/SOLID(not outlined) RAISED WHITE LETTERS</B>.  Suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000016||Raydar||02-04-2000||11:05 AM||||Scott, I used BF Goodrich Radial T/As for years. Always have had good luck with them. Good ride, decent handling, exceptional treadwear. <BR>The last set I put on my Formula were Goodyear Eagle GT-IIs. Never again. They absolutely SUCK! They won't balance. They're noisy. Tried Eagle HPs before that. They *were out of round*! (Goodyear ATE those.) The next ones I buy will likely be BF Goodrich.||||reg||1
Z||000017||2birds||02-04-2000||11:00 PM||||I have had the BF Goodrich Comp T/A HR4 on my 86 GT for four years now.  225/60/15 rear and 215/60/15 front.  I'm about to replace them after 40,000 miles, which is right in line with the warranty.  I paid $68 a piece for mine, and I like them.  They wear twice as well as the OEM Eagle GT's, which, by the way, were not speed rated.  I used to go through rear tires in 20,000 miles until I put in the poly bushings and sway bar.<BR>I had trouble with handling, too, when I had mismatched tire brands once.  I tried an S-rated store brand on back, and my older H-rated unidirectionals up front.  The car felt like it wanted to swap ends until I put the old ones on the back again.  The tire store (Discount) was cool about the whole thing.  They even verified my alignment was in spec for free, after giving me a refund.||||reg||1
Z||000018||lowCG||02-06-2000||07:54 PM||||I had a set of YokoA509s on my last car,stuck like glue in all conditions except snow and ice,but they wear like they're made out of foam rubber or something,Bridgestone RE71s are the same way.I now have Firestone SZ50s,a lot of tire for the money,ride alot quieter than my old Dunlop D60s,wear decent,and definately have an agressive tread pattern,however that wasn't a consideration in my purchase.This tire,I understand,uses some technologies from much spendier tires.Actually I have a good friend in the tire business who always hooks me up with the most for the $,he uses these on some of his vehicles as well,and yes,he has to pay for them.They cost right around $100 a piece in my size(205,225-16).||||reg||1
Z||000019||Shiner||02-06-2000||09:31 PM||||Thanks to all of you for your responses  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>After a lot of research on the net, I think I will go with BFGoodrich Comp T/A VR4, 215/60/15 up front and 225/60/15 in the rear.  I definatly won't exceed the speed rating (V, which is up to 149, if I remember correctly) with a 2.8l.  They look pretty good (not BOSS, but decent), they have GREAT tread wear for their class, they should be alright in the rain, and they are supposed to be pretty aggresive tires.  A set in the sizes up above would set me back just under four bills from The Tirerack, delivered to my door.  I also know a few people who have had very good experiences with BFG tires.  <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000020||Eric||02-07-2000||12:11 AM||||Everything lowCG said about the A509s is true.  Backs are nearly toast with 3k miles.  I'll probably go through two more sets of rear tires before the fronts need to go.  I think I might get some bigger wheels and use those tires (rubber softener and shaved tread, legal?  Who cares?) and stock wheels as autoX combination cause I definatly don't want to buy tires that soft again for the road.  They also suck in snow.  I get stuck all the time in hardpacked snow less than 1" deep!  They also throw the backend loose as I go through periodic snow patches on the road.  It gets scary at times, but I'm a real tough guy, so I don't cry.||||reg||1
Z||000021||DRH||02-07-2000||03:01 AM||||My 88 Formula came with Bridgestone RE71s with a decent amount of tread left. <BR>Not sure of total miles but I put 8k on and the tread seems to go fast. Backs are to the wear bar, fronts are half gone. <P>They do stick great on dry, good wet, and look great but I think I'll try something with a treadwear rating higher than 140 next time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh... They are worse than useless on snow also... on downhill the brake accelerates faster than the gas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
